# const_ns_qualified_visibility_rejected.tkr — the CHECKER-STAGE compile-fail fold host.
#
# The project it names is still here and still asserted (`m1::PRIV`, #594 crumb 8c / issue #613),
# and it now carries the `member_const_visibility_leak_rejected` regression too (`leakbase`/
# `leaksub`, #594 crumb 7) — that project became the PARSE-stage host and can no longer reach the
# checker at all, so its assertion had to move to a build that does. Around those two, THIRTY-FIVE
# further rejected constructs live one per namespace under `src/`, each a fixture that used to be a
# `Given source inline` snippet in regressor.tkr paying for its OWN failing build.
#
# WHY THEY SHARE ONE BUILD (owner ruling 2026-07-25 — "somente um build é necessário"): the runner
# already supports it. A compile-fail scenario that declares NO `Given source`/`Given source inline`
# is checked against the PROJECT's own failing build, and that build is cached across every scenario
# of this file (`tkr_compile_fail_artifact` → `tkr_ensure_built`). The compiler's item walk reports
# EVERY diagnostic it finds rather than stopping at the first, so one build emits all thirty-seven
# and each scenario below pins its own. Measured, not assumed: 37 scenarios, 1 build.
#
# WHY FOUR ARE NOT FOLDED (the `cases/*.tks` rows at the end). Their diagnostics come from a checker
# PRE-WALK pass, which still stops at the FIRST error instead of collecting — folding one of them in
# masks all thirty-seven behind it. Each was found by measurement (build, see which diagnostic is
# alone, park it, repeat), not by guessing. They cost one build each until the pre-walk passes
# collect like the item walk does; that is the residual, and it is four builds, not sixty-five.
#
# WHY THE `F8 target` ROWS ARE NOT FOLDED EITHER: their subject IS the build configuration
# (`TEKO_TARGET=<unsupported>`), and a differing configuration is a different build by construction.
Feature: the two cross-namespace visibility rejections, in one build

  Scenario: the root namespace may not read another namespace's private const, even namespace-qualified
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "is private to"

  Scenario: a subclass's own namespace may not read a private const inherited from its base's namespace
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "member const `SECRET` is private to `Base`"

